  "summary": "Technical illustrations showing assembled objects with components separated and arranged around the whole.",
  "description": "Characterized by components of an object shown separated from their assembled positions with connecting lines indicating relationships, created to facilitate assembly and repair instructions. Popularized during mid-century manufacturing boom, particularly in automotive and appliance industries, this technique improved clarity in complex technical documentation.",
  "culturalContext": "Exploded-view technical illustration — components floated apart along assembly axes — evolved from Renaissance dissection drawings and 19th-century patent drawings into the 20th-century manual's signature genre: Haynes car manuals, IKEA wordless assembly, LEGO instructions, Apple's device teardowns. Isometric dot-and-line discipline (Technical Illustration Office standards, wartime aviation manuals) made it a universal engineering language readable without shared words.",
